lesson:
An **interface** is a promise: "anything that is an `IDamageable` has a `Health` and a `TakeDamage` method". It has no code of its own, only the shape.

```
public interface IDamageable
{
    int Health { get; }
    bool IsDestroyed { get; }
    void TakeDamage(int amount);
}

public class Crate : IDamageable { ... }
public class ArmoredWall : IDamageable { ... }
```

**Why bother?** Because now an explosion doesn't care what it hits:

```
foreach (IDamageable target in targets)
    target.TakeDamage(30);
```

A crate, a wall, a player, a tree: if it signs the contract, the explosion works on it. Tomorrow you add a `Barrel` and the explosion code doesn't change at all. This is called **programming to an interface**: write code against the promise, not the concrete class.

Names start with `I` by convention: `IDamageable`, `IComparable<T>`, `IEnumerable<T>`. A class can implement **many** interfaces (`class Player : IDamageable, IHealable`), which is a big reason to prefer interfaces over one deep family tree.

**Built-in interfaces plug your classes into .NET.** If `Score` implements `IComparable<Score>`, then `list.Sort()` knows how to sort scores:

```
public int CompareTo(Score other)
{
    int byPoints = other.Points.CompareTo(Points);   // higher first
    if (byPoints != 0) return byPoints;
    return Seconds.CompareTo(other.Seconds);          // then faster first
}
```

`CompareTo` returns a negative number if this one comes first, 0 for a tie, positive if it comes after. The common mistake is getting the direction backwards: `Points.CompareTo(other.Points)` sorts lowest first. Swap the two sides to sort high scores first.

**Interfaces make code testable.** A `StockAlert` that needs an `INotifier` can be given a real SMS sender in the clinic, an email sender in the office, or a fake that just records messages in a test. In Unity you'll call `other.GetComponent<IDamageable>()` on whatever a bullet hit, and it works for every kind of target.

example.code:
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public interface IDamageable
{
    int Health { get; }
    void TakeDamage(int amount);
}

public class Crate : IDamageable
{
    public int Health { get; private set; } = 10;
    public void TakeDamage(int amount) => Health = Math.Max(0, Health - amount);
}

public class Player : IDamageable
{
    public int Health { get; private set; } = 100;
    public int Shield { get; private set; } = 20;
    public void TakeDamage(int amount)
    {
        int soaked = Math.Min(Shield, amount);      // the shield takes the hit first
        Shield -= soaked;
        Health = Math.Max(0, Health - (amount - soaked));
    }
}

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        var hit = new List<IDamageable> { new Crate(), new Player() };
        foreach (IDamageable t in hit) t.TakeDamage(30);
        foreach (IDamageable t in hit) Console.WriteLine($"{t.GetType().Name}: {t.Health}");
    }
}

